<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Traprain Law</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Traprain Law is a hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-lothian">East Lothian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, crowned by one of the largest hillforts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. A centre of the Votadini from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> through the Roman period, it received Roman goods in quantity, and in 1919 excavators found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traprain-law-treasure">Traprain Law Treasure</a>, a hoard of cut Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> buried in the early 5th century. It was largely abandoned afterwards, as power shifted to sites such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/traprain-law.jpg" alt="Traprain Law" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Danieljksn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traprain_Law_Aerial.webm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Traprain Law rises about 220 metres above sea level, 6 km east of Haddington. Like nearby Berwick Law and the Bass Rock it is made of phonolite, an unusual rock intruded about 350 million years ago into the sandstones and limestones of the Carboniferous Ballagan Formation. The name Traprain Law dates only from the late 18th century, after a local hamlet; earlier maps call the hill Dunpendyrlaw, and fishermen who use it as a landmark still call it Dunpelder, perhaps the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbric">Cumbric</a> for fort of the spear shafts. In 1547 a beacon was placed on it to warn of an English invasion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The hill was a place of burial by about 1500 BCE and shows signs of occupation and ramparts after 1000 BCE; the ramparts were rebuilt and realigned many times, and at its maximum extent the fort covered about 16 hectares. Excavations show occupation in the Late Iron Age from about 40 CE until the last quarter of the 2nd century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> recorded the Votadini here in the 1st century, and the site is generally identified with Curia, named by Ptolemy. It is thought to have been the capital of the kingdom of Gododdin before the seat moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/din-eidyn">Din Eidyn</a>, the Castle Rock of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>.</p><p>A team led by Curle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a> excavated from 1914 to 1923, and in 1919 Alexander Ormiston Curle recovered the treasure from a pit: over 24 kg of hacked Roman silver, mostly table ware but also early Christian items and parts of an officer's uniform, some perhaps from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Ravenna, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Coins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadius">Arcadius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> date it after the Roman departure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Scholars have proposed raiding plunder or payment for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> service as its origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The treasure is in the care of the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. A quarry for road stone, leased to the district council in 1938, has scarred part of the hill. In legend it was the cliff from which Thenaw, mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kentigern">Saint Mungo</a>, was thrown by her father, King Lot.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Traprain Law” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hillforts such as <b>Traprain Law</b> and Edinburgh Castle Rock were key centres.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gododdin">Kingdom of Gododdin</a></li><li><blockquote>They descended from Iron Age communities of Lothian and the Borders, and their great hillfort at <b>Traprain Law</b> in East Lothian was a major centre from before the Roman arrival in the 80s.</blockquote><a href="/state/votadini">Votadini</a></li></ul></section>
